      "id": "51e5044f3de5312ca1a31d412fa46907900ce8272acd7feb5b5157f52f896f11",
      "title": "UCLA Library Special Collections (LSC) Research Fellowships 2027 (Funded)",
      "url": "https://opportunitydesk.org/2026/11/12/ucla-library-special-collections-research-fellowships-2027",
      "category": "opportunities",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-21T17:59:11.193Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://www.library.ucla.edu/about/programs/library-special-collections-lsc-research-fellowships/",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "UCLA Library Special Collections Research Fellowships | UCLA Library",
      "pageDescription": "The Library provides funding for on-site graduate-level, post-doctoral or independent research with Library Special Collections materials.",
      "evidenceText": "UCLA Library Special Collections Research Fellowships | UCLA Library UCLA Library Special Collections Research Fellowships The Library provides funding for on-site graduate-level, post-doctoral or independent research with Library Special Collections materials. AskLSC@library.ucla.edu (310) 825-4988 Apply Now Apply Now More Information About the Program UCLA Library Special Collections (LSC) Research Fellowships provide one-time funding disbursements to help defray travel and other research-related expenses for projects that would significantly benefit from on-site research. The call for applications opens in the mid- to late fall quarter, and awardees are notified in early January. LSC prioritizes applications from those working toward a discrete project, publication or research using collections representing marginalized and stigmatized groups. Fellowships are not limited to scholarly research: LSC encourages applications from artists, activists and individuals engaged in inquiry using primary source materials. Fellowships must be completed within the awarded calendar year, and fellows will be asked to create or participate in an outreach deliverable around their research and project. Awards are disbursed as reimbursements following the research visit, and payment can take up to three months. Current UCLA affiliates or anyone affiliated with UCLA in the past two years should expect additional wait times. Key Dates November 1, 2026: Application period opens December 1, 2026: Complete application packets due January 2027: Successful applicants notified Eligibility Eligibility is limited to United States citizens and permanent residents with the legal right to work in the U.S. Applicants may apply to multiple fellowships, but each application requires a separate application form. Fellowships may support research using all processed collections and materials administered by UCLA Library Special Collections, though some fellowships support subject-specific research. Application Instructions To apply for an LSC Research Fellowship, please complete a fellowship application form (opens in a new tab) and include the following required documents: An abbreviated CV (under two pages) A project proposal that is under two pages and includes the title and project format; a general description or abstract of the project; a detailed explanation of how materials stewarded by UCLA Library Special Collections are essential to the progress or completion of the project Contact information for one reference who can speak on the applicant’s work, proposed project or their research. Available Fellowships James and Sylvia Thayer Short-Term Research Fellowships Thayer fellowships provide support for research in any collections administered by UCLA Library Special Collections, with preference to students enrolled in a graduate program at UCLA. Multiple annual fellowships will be awarded in the amount of $3000 to facilitate research lasting one to three months. Awards are funded by an endowment established by longtime UCLA benefactors James and Sylvia Thayer. Thayer fellows are required to create a public-facing outreach deliverable discussing their project and research conducted in Special Collections. Past deliverables have included creating a series of social media posts or stories, creating didactic text and images for a pop-up digital exhibit or providing a short lecture and/or workshop. Kenneth Karmiole Endowed Research Fellowship in UCLA Library Special Collections The Karmiole fellowship supports the use of UCLA Library Special Collections materials by visiting scholars and graduate students. One annual fellowship will be awarded in the amount of $5000 to allow a scholar to pursue research lasting from one to three months. The award is funded by an endowment established by Kenneth Karmiole, an internationally renowned antiquarian bookseller who earned his master’s degree in library science from UCLA in 1971. The benefactor made previous gifts to the UCLA Library for the Kenneth Karmiole Endowment for Rare Books and Manuscripts, the William Andrews Clark Memorial Library and the UCLA Graduate School of Education and Information Studies. He has owned and operated Kenneth Karmiole, Bookseller Inc. (opens in a new tab) since 1976. Karmiole fellows are required to meet with the donor during their visit as well as participate in a public-facing program to discuss their project and the research conducted in Special Collections. Barbara Rootenberg Short-term Research Fellowship in the History of Medicine and the Life Sciences The Rootenberg fellowship promotes the use of materials in History & Special Collections for the Sciences in UCLA Library Special Collections. One annual fellowship is awarded in the amount of $1000 to allow a scholar to pursue research lasting from two to six weeks. The award is named for Barbara Rootenberg, an alumna of the UCLA School of Library Science and an internationally renowned antiquarian bookseller. Rootenberg fellows are required to create a public-facing outreach deliverable discussing their project and research conducted in Special Collections. Past deliverables have included creating a series of social media posts or stories, creating didactic text and images for a pop-up digital exhibit or providing a short lecture and/or workshop. Past Research Fellows Please view our PDF list of Past Research Fellows at UCLA Library Special Collections (2004-present) (opens in a new tab) for past fellows, their fellowship project titles and the products in which their fellowship research was used. Have Further Questions? Contact Library Special Collections for more information. Email Us Email Us Associated News article Fellowship facilitates original research and inquiry October 8, 2024 The Karmiole Endowed Research Fellowship at the UCLA Library advances scholarship for future generations article The Queen of Comedy-Variety: Exploring the Carol Burnett Papers August 1, 2024 2024 James and Sylvia Thayer Short-Term Research Fellowship recipient Jessica Hoover explores gender and women performers in comedy-variety television using LSC's Carol Burnett Papers See More Associated Articles",
