      "finalUrl": "https://www.iie.org/programs/oceanfuturesfellowship/",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "The Ocean Futures Fellowship: Building Ocean Career Pathways | IIE",
      "pageDescription": "The Ocean Futures Fellowship is a six-month program that equips emerging ocean professionals with the skills, experience, and networks to lead in ocean science and conservation. Fellows engage in hands-on projects, mentorship, and global collaboration to drive ocean literacy and impact.",
      "evidenceText": "The Ocean Futures Fellowship: Building Ocean Career Pathways | IIE Home » Programs » The Ocean Futures Fellowship: Building Ocean Career Pathways The Ocean Futures Fellowship: Building Ocean Career Pathways The Ocean Futures Fellowship: Building Ocean Career Pathways Overview The Ocean Futures Fellowship empowers the next generation of ocean professionals, equipping them to address pressing ocean challenges while strengthening their own career pathways. Through training, educational projects, professional development and mentorship over a twelve-month program, Fellows will gain advanced skills, hands-on experience, and draw on OceanX’s international connections that not only build their career, but foster ocean literacy and leadership on a global scale. The Fellowship program contributes to the goals of the UN Ocean Decade and SDG 14 by cultivating the next generation of ocean leaders who will drive innovation, inspire global audiences, and expand ocean literacy to foster ocean protection. Fellows will receive a monthly stipend and will participate fully remotely from their home countries. The program includes the potential opportunity to travel on board the OceanXplorer vessel or attend an OceanX Summit. * Learn more about current Fellows and OceanX Capstone Project A cornerstone of the Ocean Futures Fellowship experience is the capstone project. The capstone project is intended to integrate learning from each Fellow’s specific area of focus and combine it with ocean research and technology, as well as additional learning alongside other Fellows within OceanX. The capstone project will showcase the Fellow’s ability to lead collaboratively, think systemically, and mobilize others toward a shared goal of ocean protection. Capstone projects must contribute meaningfully to ocean health, ocean action & awareness, sustainable ocean production and/or protecting marine biodiversity. Throughout the fellowship, Fellows will have access to mentors from the OceanX Changemakers Community which includes early career to established ocean and creative development professionals from around the world. Fellows will receive mentorship and feedback on Fellows’ learning goals and capstone project. Areas of Focus The Ocean Futures Fellowship seeks applicants that demonstrate experience and/or a strong interest in one of the following Fellowship focus areas, as well as knowledge and interest in AI literacy and applying emerging technologies responsibly and effectively to the sustainability of the ocean. The Fellowship requires a significant time commitment. Fellows must be able to participate fully in scheduled program activities, including training, mentorship sessions, and the capstone project. Applicants should carefully consider their other commitments before applying. Applicants who are full-time students or working in full-time employment opportunities are not eligible for the fellowship. Fellowship Duration The Digital Asset & Archives, Immersive Media, and Partnerships Fellows will begin their 12-month fellowship on February 15, 2027 – February 11, 2028. The AI and Marketing Communications Fellows will begin their 12-month fellowship in early April 2027 – Late March 2028. 1. AI Fellow – Gain experience in designing and applying multimodal AI systems in collaboration with OceanX team members to accelerate ocean science insights and learn how AI can accelerate ocean science insight and support exploration and conservation storytelling. Start Date: April 2027 Fellowship Overview: The Ocean Futures Fellow – AI will collaborate with OceanX team members across data, science, education, and communications to gain experience to learn how AI-enabled workflows are designed, built, and evaluated in an ocean science context that advances OceanX’s mission: bringing impactful ocean exploration, discovery, and conservation stories to the world, and accelerating the ability of ocean science to generate insight from complex data. This Fellowship is intentionally designed to gain experience with Scientific AI R&D , with a focus on developing and/or applying models that can jointly reason across multiple modalities relevant to ocean science such as imagery, acoustics, 3D seafloor mapping/bathymetry, sequenced eDNA, and chemical and physical water property measurements, to support research, discovery, and scientific understanding. Project Focus Areas: Multimodal Model Development : Collaborate to develop and/or apply models that jointly reason across multiple ocean-relevant modalities, including (but not limited to) visual data, acoustic data, 3D bathymetry/seafloor mapping, sequenced eDNA, and chemical/physical water properties. Model Training Experience : Gain design training approach experience by running experiments, including dataset preparation, training pipelines, and iterative model development. Data & Dataset Development : Learn dataset curation, cleaning, labeling, and design through guided exercises with domain experts; collaborate with domain experts to ensure scientific validity and utility. Experimentation & Validation : Gain experience to define evaluation protocols and benchmarks to assess model performance, generalization, and robustness; communicate results clearly to technical and non-technical audiences. Cross-Functional Scientific Collaboration : Partner with scientists, engineers, and expedition teams to translate scientific needs into applied ML problems and to translate model outputs into actionable scientific insight. 2. Digital Asset & Archives Fellow – Gain experience in learning archival and digital asset management practices alongside OceanX’s media, science, and production teams. Start Date: February 15, 2027 Fellowship Overview: The Ocean Futures Fellow – Digital Asset & Archives will collaborate with OceanX’s media, science, and production teams to organize, maintain, and enhance access to OceanX’s growing media archive. The Fellow will learn how media assets are cataloged, made searchable, and prepared for preservation. Project Focus Areas: Digital Asset Management & Archiving : Gain skills in the ingestion, organization, cataloging, and maintenance of OceanX media assets, including video, stills, audio, and documentation. Metadata & Taxonomy Support : Learn metadata standards through hands-on exercises Media Operations Support : Gain exposure to how assets are tracked across projects. Archive Readiness & Preservation : Learn how archive readiness reviews identify documentation, rights, and technical gaps 3. Immersive Media Fellow – Gain experience using technology, such as virtual reality, augmented reality, game design, and UX/UI, to engage audiences with ocean exploration and education. Start Date: February 15, 2027 Fellowship Overview: The Ocean Futures Fellow – Immersive Media will collaborate with both the OceanX Education and OceanX Singapore teams to learn how innovative programming is designed to further the goals of OceanX to increase ocean literacy. The Fellow will draw on their passion, creativity and experience with ocean science and conservation and/or ocean literacy to develop prototype content as part of their learning and capstone work. The Fellow will also explore and apply emerging technologies, including artificial intelligence (AI), to enhance storytelling, learning design, audience engagement, and content development across OceanX platforms. Project Focus Areas: Content Development : Utilize experience, creativity and passion for ocean conservation and literacy to engage with OceanX Education’s team, partners, and developers to create immersive media initiatives for a variety of external stakeholders. Gain skills to leverage AI-enabled tools (e.g., generative media, text, image, video, or audio tools) to support ideation, prototyping, content iteration, and learning asset development while adhering to OceanX ethical and brand standards. Design Process Experience : Focus on user interface and experience and experiment with AI-supported design workflows, such as rapid prototyping, audience testing, accessibility enhancements, and personalized learning experiences. Project Management : Learn program and content management practices; document AI use cases as part of reflective learning. Public Programming : Observe and learn how public programming is planned and delivered, and contribute ideas. Gain skills to support the integration of interactive and AI-enhanced elements into public programming, exhibitions, and live or hybrid experiences where appropriate. 4. Marketing and Communications Fellow – Collaborate to plan and execute strategic, cross‑channel campaigns while gaining experience in content creation, digital communications, and collaborative projects. Start Date: April 2027 Fellowship Overview: The Ocean Futures Fellow – Marketing & Communications will collaborate with the OceanX’s MarComms team to gain skills to learn how strategic campaigns are planned, created, and executed that advance ocean science, exploration, and conservation. This Fellow will gain hands-on experience across multiple marketing functions including content creation, campaign coordination, digital communications, and cross-functional project support. The fellowship provides exposure to strategic planning, storytelling, and audience engagement across diverse channels and stakeholders—from scientists and educators to partners and the public. The Fellow will gain exposure to how campaigns advance ocean science and conservation. Project Focus Areas: Campaign & Content Support : Learn how integrated campaigns are developed and executed. Copywriting & Storytelling : Build copywriting skills through practice assignments and mentor feedback, and learn OceanX’s brand voice. 5. Partnerships Fellow – Gain experience in establishing and maintaining relationships with potential OceanX partners. Start Date: February 15, 2027 Fellowship Summary: The Ocean Futures Fellow – Partnerships will collaborate with the OceanX’s Partnerships team across New York and Singapore to gain experience in the establishment and continuation of relationships with potential partners. The Fellow will gain understanding of the full lifecycle of partnerships and fundraising efforts, including prospect research, proposal and presentation development, CRM and pipeline management, and coordination of partner engagement activities and events. The Fellow will engage with senior leadership and external partners across OceanX, while offering the opportunity to learn OceanX’s approach to projects that have a tangible impact on our mission to shape a sustainable ocean. Project Focus Areas: Fundraising Research and Development : Gain experience in developing an AI-supported workflow for identifying and researching prospective donors and grant opportunities. Gain experience in AI-supported donor research by developing a prototype workflow and design a prototype partner communications approach Targeted Partner Communications: Gain experience as the liaison between the Marketing and Partnerships team to design and launch a more targeted, AI-enabled partner communications system. Gain skills to segment partners by interests and program areas, develop tailored content and automated communication workflows, and pilot quarterly outreach to the organization’s partner ecosystem. Partnerships Team Process and Project Flow: Design a model process as a learning project that supports the tracking of team activities and the production of materials that showcase the team’s work to the broader organization. CRM & Pipeline Management : Learn CRM and pipeline management practices through guided, supervised exercises Event & Engagement Coordination : Learn how partner events are planned and run through observation and guided practice Program Timeline, Location, and Support Estimated Timeline October 19, 2026: Application Opens November 17, 2026: Application Closes at 11:59 PM U.S. Eastern Time Early to Mid-December 2026: Applicant Interviews Late December 2026 – Early January 2027: Announcement of Fellowship Awards February 15, 2027: Digital Assets & Archives, Immersive Media, and Partnerships 12-month fellowships Begin Early April 2027: AI and Marketing Communications 12-month fellowships begin Location Fellows will participate in the fellowship remotely from their home countries throughout the twelve-month fellowship period. Fellows participate in scheduled program sessions, mentor meetings, and training. To make these sessions possible, Fellows should be able to join scheduled sessions that fall within SGT business hours. Note, the Marketing and Communications, Digital Assets & Archives , and Partnerships Fellows have flexibility to collaborate with team members located in SGT business hours or New York (ET) business hours for the duration of the twelve-month fellowship. Session times are set in advance in coordination with Fellows. Stipend Fellows will receive a monthly stipend to support their participation in the program. This stipend  does not include additional allowances for housing, relocation, transportation, dependents, or other personal expenses. A technology stipend is available for select Fellows on a case-by-case basis. Eligibility To be eligible to apply, applicants must meet the following criteria: Be at least 21 years old. Able to commit and participate fully in the 12-month fellowship beginning either from February or April 2027. The expectation of the Ocean Futures Fellowship is that it will be a participant’s primary professional and learning commitment during the fellowship period. Full-time students or individuals working in full-time employment opportunities are not eligible. Hold a bachelor’s degree from an accredited four-year institution prior to the start of the fellowship Individuals applying for the Immersive Media Fellowship or Marketing Communications Fellowship must have a degree related to ocean science, ocean conservation, media, communications, or other related fields. Individuals applying for the Partnerships Fellowship must have a degree related to sustainable finance, policy, science, nonprofit management, media, communications, or business adjacent fields. Full professional proficiency in English (C2-level or equivalent) Practical knowledge and interest in AI literacy and applying emerging technologies responsibly and effectively Motivated by purpose, access, mentorship, and global opportunity. Application Process Applicants must complete an online application which includes: General background information (e.g. contact information and details about your current and previous experience related to ocean education) Resume/CV Responses to short essay questions Short video outlining your qualifications and motivations for becoming an Ocean Futures Fellow Important:  Before starting the application, please have the following links ready to enter: Link to your resume/CV (required) Link to your video response (required) Links to your sample project and English score (optional) The application is now closed. Please check back for future application updates. Questions Please email oceanfuturesfellowship@iie.org",
