      "pageTitle": "Brazil: Top electoral court fines Meta $1.3 million over failing to fully comply with judicial orders",
      "pageDescription": "The fine came after President Lula&#x27;s coalition filed a complaint. The court said there was no evidence of intent to influence the election. Meta said it will defend itself against &quot;false allegations.&quot;",
      "evidenceText": "Brazil: Electoral court fines Meta $1.3M for noncompliance Skip to content Skip to main menu Skip to more DW sites In focus COP31 AI Ukraine war Latest audio Latest videos Live TV Advertisement https://p.dw.com/p/5PL2q Prefer DW on Google The fine was based on Meta's overall handling of the court orders Image: Thibault Camus/AP Photo/picture alliance Advertisement Brazil's top electoral court has ordered Meta to pay 7.5 million reais ($1.34 million; €1.22 million) for failing to fully comply with judicial orders during last month's presidential election. However, the judicial body has so far found no evidence that Meta deliberately acted to influence the election outcome. Ahead of the runoff vote on October 25, leftist President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva's coalition filed a complaint, accusing Meta of \"algorithmic manipulation\" favoring his far-right rival  Flavio Bolsonaro. Platforms owned by Meta include Facebook , Instagram and WhatsApp. Meta told the court last month that it had implemented measures to guarantee equal treatment for both campaigns. Meta lacked transparency, court says The Superior Electoral Court (TSE) unanimously ruled on Tuesday to impose the 7.5 million reais fine on the US tech giant. Meta missed deadlines and lacked transparency in its investigation, the court said. On the other hand, the court's reporting judge Marco Aurelio Belizze wrote in his ruling that there were no preliminary evidence indicating that any intentional actions were taken to influence the election. \"That ​conclusion would require robust evidence, to be produced through the appropriate legal proceedings,\" he said. In his victory speech, Lula reiterated that Brazil would 'not accept being anyone’s backyard,' a recurring campaign message defending the country’s sovereignty against US influence Image: Natacha Pisarenko/AP Photo/picture alliance According to Bellizze, the fine was based on Meta's overall handling of the court orders, including delays, incomplete disclosures, and challenges in verifying some of the information it provided. In a statement on Wednesday, Meta strongly denied the court's ruling. \"We will continue to present the facts and defend ourselves against false allegations in the ‌ongoing ​legal proceedings,\" Meta said in a statement on Wednesday. Lula vows to rein in social media Lula defeated Bolsonaro in a tight run-off in October 25 with him winning 51.7% of the vote against Bolsonaro's 48.2%. In his victory speech, Lula urged more regulation of big tech platforms after allegations that Meta interfered in the race. \"We had to overcome foreign interference in the election, coming from outside through digital platforms that we will have to regulate,\" he said. Advertisement Skip next section Explore more Explore more Brazil's Lula celebrates suprise election victory Brazil's Lula celebrates suprise election victory Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva has won a historic fourth term after defeating far-right rival Flavio Bolsonaro in a runoff election. It's a remarkable political comeback for Lula who trailed in the first round. Politics 10/26/2026 October 26, 2026 13:28 min Flavio Bolsonaro flees to the US from Brazil Flavio Bolsonaro flees to the US from Brazil After losing the runoff in Brazil's presidential election, Flavio Bolsonaro has left his home country and arrived in Miami, Florida. The politician faces charges of corruption and money laundering back home. Politics 10/28/2026 October 28, 2026 05:32 min Skip next section Similar stories Similar stories Italy slaps Meta with €805mn fine over access to Android photos Italian regulators say Meta accessed photos and from Italian users via the Facebook application for Android devices. Technology 11/14/2026 November 14, 2026 US judge jails Indian national in foiled Sikh separatist murder plot New Delhi has denied any role in the plot to kill Sikh separatist Gurpatwant Singh Pannun in New York. DW has more. Politics 11/21/2026 November 21, 2026 