      "pageTitle": "Introducing Spotlight 2.0! Now with an AI research crew for your investigations. The main problem with the previous version was that a single agent handled the research. Each search followed the… | Tom Vaillant",
      "pageDescription": "Introducing Spotlight 2.0! Now with an AI research crew for your investigations.\n\nThe main problem with the previous version was that a single agent handled the research. Each search followed the last, so it got lost in its own bias. Early finds were saved but slipped out of its context as the case went on, and a lot of clues fell through the cracks.\n\nNow a swarm of agents can go down several different directions and surface far more leads. A decision model triages them so you can pick which clues to pursue. The investigator agent chases those clues and a separate fact-checker tests what it finds, in cycles. At the end an analyst agent proposes what goes into your wiki.\n\nAfter three weeks of trial and error the local version now fits in 24GB of RAM (still early days). It",
      "evidenceText": "Introducing Spotlight 2.0! Now with an AI research crew for your investigations. The main problem with the previous version was that a single agent handled the research. Each search followed the… | Tom Vaillant Agree & Join LinkedIn By clicking Continue to join or sign in, you agree to LinkedIn’s User Agreement , Privacy Policy , and Cookie Policy . Skip to main content Tom Vaillant 2w Edited Report this post Introducing Spotlight 2.0! Now with an AI research crew for your investigations. The main problem with the previous version was that a single agent handled the research. Each search followed the last, so it got lost in its own bias. Early finds were saved but slipped out of its context as the case went on, and a lot of clues fell through the cracks. Now a swarm of agents can go down several different directions and surface far more leads. A decision model triages them so you can pick which clues to pursue. The investigator agent chases those clues and a separate fact-checker tests what it finds, in cycles. At the end an analyst agent proposes what goes into your wiki. After three weeks of trial and error the local version now fits in 24GB of RAM (still early days). It's been a balancing act, give the small model too many rules it gets stuck, too few and it wanders off. But it turns out small models work better as a team! So I hired some extra help: - Qwen3.5-9B, post-trained to do the agents' work - a 2B decision model, post-trained to help steer the investigation and sort the leads - Gemma E4B, which turns scraped pages and long conversations into short summaries With the crew on your machine you can hand an agent your sensitive cases. Indicator Lab members can install it from the Lab app and run their investigations through an AI subscription or locally on Osaurus. Case study in comments! https://lnkd.in/eMbR8Yg7 …more 36 2 Comments Like Comment Share Copy LinkedIn Facebook X Tom Vaillant 2w Report this comment For the technically intrigued, the full faffing journey is here: https://spotlight.buriedsignals.com/going-sovereign / Like Reply 2 Reactions 3 Reactions Lars Adrian Giske 2w Report this comment Great work! Like Reply 1 Reaction 2 Reactions See more comments To view or add a comment, sign in 6,225 followers 175 Posts 1 Article View Profile Follow More from this author The newsroom intelligence layer: internal context, external products Tom Vaillant 4mo Explore content categories Career Productivity Finance Soft Skills & Emotional Intelligence Project Management Education Technology Leadership Ecommerce User Experience Show more Show less Sign in to view more content Create your free account or sign in to continue your search Email or phone Password Show Forgot password? Sign in Sign in with Email or New to LinkedIn? Join now By clicking Continue to join or sign in, you agree to LinkedIn’s User Agreement , Privacy Policy , and Cookie Policy .",
